// vyto/crypto/openssl — OpenSSL bindings: TLS, X.509, and the primitives the
// pure-Vyto modules do not cover.
//
// STATUS: STUB. Every entry point below panics. The shapes are here so callers
// and the shim can be designed against something concrete, and so the decisions
// that are expensive to reverse — package layout, provisioning, error handling,
// object ownership — are settled before the first line of C is written. Nothing
// in this file links anything yet; there is deliberately no extern "C" block
// and no #link, so importing it costs nothing and fails loudly rather than
// silently returning wrong bytes.
//
// The reason this exists is TLS. vyto/net gets HTTPS today by linking libcurl,
// which is a whole HTTP client dragged in for its TLS; a raw `Socket` cannot be
// wrapped. Everything else here follows from already having libssl linked.
//
// ---- why its own package directory ----
//
// A native/src is compiled once per package *directory*, for every module in
// it. This lives in lib/vyto/crypto/openssl/ rather than beside crypto.vt so a
// program that hashes a password does not link libcrypto, exactly as
// vyto/crypto/ecc is split from vyto/crypto and vyto/util/uuid from vyto/util
// (uuid.vt:20-24). Three packages under vyto/crypto, three different native
// costs: none, micro-ecc, OpenSSL.
//
// ---- relationship to the pure-Vyto modules ----
//
// This does NOT replace vyto/crypto or vyto/crypto/argon2, and it must not
// become the default path for what they already do. They have no dependency,
// cross-compile with nothing to provision, and are already vector-tested. The
// rule to hold to:
//
//   Use the pure-Vyto module unless you need something it does not have.
//
// What only OpenSSL can give: TLS, X.509 certificate parsing and chain
// verification, RSA, Ed25519/X25519, AES (which is a bad idea in software
// without AES-NI — cache-timing leaks — and a good idea through OpenSSL, which
// uses the instruction), SHA-3, and hardware-accelerated hashing when
// throughput actually matters.
//
// Where both implement the same primitive (SHA-256, HMAC, HKDF, PBKDF2,
// ChaCha20-Poly1305, P-256 ECDSA/ECDH), the two MUST agree byte for byte, and
// the test suite must prove it rather than assume it. Two implementations of
// SHA-256 already coexist in this tree — pure Vyto in crypto.vt and C in
// ecc_shim.c — and examples/86_crypto.vt holds both to the same vectors for
// precisely this reason. Add OpenSSL as a third column there, not as a
// replacement column.
//
// ---- provisioning, the part that decides everything else ----
//
// POSIX links the system library: `#link "crypto"`, `#link "ssl"`. That is how
// vyto/net already handles libcurl (net.vt), and it means a build-time
// dependency on the distro's libssl-dev.
//
// Windows has no system OpenSSL, so it needs the vendored-DLL arrangement that
// lib/vyto/net/native/provision-curl.sh already implements: headers generated
// into native/<triple>/include, the DLL supplied out of band and shipped with a
// Vyto release, never committed (*.dll is gitignored). A provision-openssl.sh
// written on that template is the intended path. Note this is the OPPOSITE of
// how micro-ecc is handled next door — that is vendored into git because it is
// small pure C and crypto tests must never silently skip. OpenSSL is neither
// small nor buildable without decisions, so it follows curl instead.
//
// Version floor: OpenSSL 3.0. The 1.1 -> 3.x soname change (libcrypto.so.1.1
// vs libcrypto.so.3) is not ABI-compatible, and supporting both means two code
// paths for the low-level APIs 3.x deprecated. Pin 3.0 and use the EVP
// interfaces throughout, which are stable across the 3.x line.
//
// Version traps worth knowing before designing around them:
//   - Argon2id via EVP_KDF exists only in 3.2+. A distro on 3.0 does not have
//     it. vyto/crypto/argon2 is pure Vyto and always present, so it stays the
//     default for password hashing regardless of what OpenSSL is installed.
//   - The FIPS provider must be loaded explicitly in 3.x, and loading it
//     removes algorithms rather than adding them. Out of scope until asked for.
//   - Engines are gone in 3.x, replaced by providers.
//
// ---- rules the implementation has to follow ----
//
// These are the ones that are painful to retrofit:
//
//  1. No OpenSSL type, constant or error code crosses into Vyto. The shim
//     translates. A version bump must not be able to change a number the Vyto
//     side compares against — the same contract regex_shim.c holds for PCRE2
//     and ecc_shim.c for micro-ecc.
//
//  2. Objects are owned by Vyto classes with `deinit`, so ARC frees the
//     SSL_CTX / EVP_PKEY / X509 the moment the handle leaves scope, the way
//     vyto/io's File and vyto/surface's Surface already do. No manual free in
//     caller code, ever.
//
//  3. The OpenSSL error queue is per-thread and stateful: an unread error from
//     one call surfaces as a bogus diagnostic on the next. The shim must drain
//     it on every entry and attach the first real reason to the failure it
//     returns. A "failed for no reason" TLS handshake is nearly always this.
//
//  4. Certificate verification is ON by default and turning it off must be
//     loud. `tls_client()` verifies the chain and the hostname; anything that
//     disables that gets a name that says so, because a TLS client that does
//     not verify is strictly worse than plaintext — it costs the same and
//     provides confidence it has not earned.
//
//  5. Failures return a result object, not a sentinel. A zero-length key or an
//     empty plaintext that a caller mistakes for success is the failure mode
//     this whole package has to avoid; vyto/crypto's AeadResult and ecc's
//     EccResult set the shape.
//
// ---- scope ----
//
// In, roughly in build order:
//   TLS client over an existing vyto/net Socket, with SNI, ALPN and hostname
//   verification; TLS server; X.509 parse, chain verify, subject/SAN/validity
//   extraction; EVP digests (SHA-2, SHA-3); EVP ciphers (AES-GCM, AES-CBC);
//   EVP_PKEY for RSA, Ed25519, X25519, and the NIST curves; PEM and DER
//   encoding; CSR generation.
//
// Out, for now:
//   FIPS provider, engines/providers beyond the default, DTLS, QUIC, OCSP
//   stapling, CMS/PKCS#7, and anything that needs a callback from C into Vyto
//   until the fn-pointer story is settled (examples/cbwrap shows the mechanism).

// ---- result shapes ----------------------------------------------------------

// A byte string that may not exist, plus the reason when it does not. Mirrors
// vyto/crypto's AeadResult and vyto/crypto/ecc's EccResult, with `err` added
// because OpenSSL failures have a diagnosable cause worth surfacing — a TLS
// handshake fails for a dozen distinct reasons and "false" helps nobody.
export class OsslResult {
    ok: bool;
    data: byte[];
    err: string;

    fn init(ok: bool, data: byte[], err: string) {
        this.ok = ok;
        this.data = data;
        this.err = err;
    }
}

